New session, so context first. This is `billing-service`, a TypeScript service on Node with Postgres. Layout is roughly `src/invoices` (calculator, repo, service), `src/payments`, `src/ledger` (customer balance entries), `src/webhooks` (inbound from the PSP and outbound to customers), `src/jobs` (queue workers) and `db/migrations`. First bug: finance says some invoice totals are off by one cent compared to what they compute by hand. Only some line items, not all. Here's the calculator:

```ts
export interface LineItem {
  description: string;
  quantity: number;
  unitPrice: number;
  taxRate: number; // 0.0825 = 8.25%
}

function round2(n: number): number {
  return Math.round(n * 100) / 100;
}

export function calculateLine(item: LineItem) {
  const subtotal = item.quantity * item.unitPrice;
  const tax = subtotal * item.taxRate;
  return {
    subtotal: round2(subtotal),
    tax: round2(tax),
    total: round2(subtotal + tax),
  };
}

export function calculateInvoice(items: LineItem[]) {
  const lines = items.map(calculateLine);
  const sum = (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  return {
    lines,
    subtotal: sum(lines.map((l) => l.subtotal)),
    tax: sum(lines.map((l) => l.tax)),
    total: sum(lines.map((l) => l.total)),
  };
}
```

My guess is floats, but I'd like you to tell me exactly where it goes wrong before we touch anything.

